+# Stock reservations — specification
+
+## Why
+
+Checkout needs to hold stock for a cart before payment. After payment it
+either confirms the hold into an order or releases it. Abandoned carts must
+not keep stock locked forever, so holds that nobody confirms lapse on their
+own. The cart service retries requests, so creating a hold must be safe to
+repeat. Each request carries an idempotency key for this.
+
+## Interface (fixed — hidden tests call exactly this)
+
+`InventoryService(repository, ledger, clock)` keeps working as is.
+
+- `InventoryService.reserve(warehouse: str, items: dict[str, int], idempotency_key: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.confirm_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Order`
+- `InventoryService.release_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.get_reservation(reservation_id: str) -> Reservation`
+- `InventoryService.available(sku, warehouse)` must take holds into account.
+- `inventory.models`: dataclass `Reservation` with `reservation_id: str`,
+  `warehouse: str`, `items: dict[str, int]` (units held per SKU),
+  `status: ReservationStatus`, `expires_at: datetime`, `order_id: str | None`;
+  enum `ReservationStatus` with members `ACTIVE`, `CONFIRMED`, `RELEASED`,
+  `EXPIRED`.
+- `inventory.errors`: new `ReservationNotFound`, `ReservationExpired`,
+  `InvalidReservationState`, `IdempotencyConflict` (all subclass
+  `InventoryError`); reuse the existing errors where they fit.
+- Time comes only from the injected clock. There is no background job: expiry
+  must take effect without anyone calling a sweep.
+
+## Terms
+
+- **Hold**: units of a SKU in a warehouse set aside by an ACTIVE, unexpired
+  reservation.
+- **Lapsed / expired**: a reservation that is still ACTIVE when
+  `clock.now() >= expires_at`. From that instant on, including the instant
+  itself, it counts as EXPIRED.
+- **Requested items**: the `items` the caller passed to `reserve`. This is
+  different from `Reservation.items`, which holds the units actually held.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Hold lifetime
+
+1. `expires_at` is the clock time at `reserve` plus the service's hold
+   duration.
+2. A service built as `InventoryService(repository, ledger, clock)` has a hold
+   duration of 20 minutes. The duration is fixed per service instance and
+   cannot be set per call. The service may offer an optional way to choose a
+   different duration at construction.
+3. When `clock.now() >= expires_at`, an ACTIVE reservation is reported with
+   status `EXPIRED` and no longer holds stock. No sweep, job or other call is
+   needed for this. Reading `get_reservation` or `available` is enough to see
+   the effect.
+4. CONFIRMED and RELEASED reservations keep their status after `expires_at`
+   passes. Only ACTIVE reservations expire.
+5. Nothing ever extends a hold or moves `expires_at` after creation, including
+   an idempotent retry.
+
+### Availability
+
+6. `available(sku, warehouse)` = on-hand − allocated − units held by ACTIVE,
+   unexpired reservations for that SKU and warehouse, floored at 0.
+7. `allocated(sku, warehouse)` still counts only PLACED orders. Holds are not
+   included. `on_hand` is unaffected by holds.
+8. `stock_report` and `StockRow` are unchanged. Their `available` column
+   reflects holds through criterion 6.
+9. Releasing a hold or its expiry makes its units available again
+   immediately.
+10. `receive` and `adjust` are never blocked by holds. If a write-off takes
+    on-hand below what is held, `available` shows 0 and the affected
+    reservations stay as they are (status and `items` unchanged).
+
+### Reserve
+
+11. Each requested line holds `min(requested, available right now)` units.
+12. A line with 0 available is left out of `Reservation.items` entirely. It is
+    not recorded with quantity 0.
+13. If at least one line holds ≥ 1 unit, `reserve` returns a new reservation:
+    status `ACTIVE`, `order_id is None`, the given `warehouse`, and `items`
+    containing only the units actually held.
+14. If no line can hold anything, `reserve` raises `InsufficientStock` and
+    creates nothing: no reservation, no hold, and the key is not consumed.
+15. Validation reuses the existing errors: an unknown warehouse raises
+    `UnknownWarehouse`, an unknown SKU raises `UnknownSku`, and a non-positive
+    or non-integer quantity raises `InvalidQuantity`. An empty `items` raises
+    some `InventoryError`.
+16. A rejected request holds nothing, not even for its valid lines, and
+    consumes no key.
+17. Reservation IDs are `RES-0001`, `RES-0002`, … in creation order. This
+    sequence is separate from order IDs.
+
+### Idempotency
+
+18. Calling `reserve` again with a key already used by a successful `reserve`,
+    with the same warehouse and the same requested items (compared as a
+    mapping, so order does not matter), returns that existing reservation in
+    its current state, whatever that state is (ACTIVE, CONFIRMED, RELEASED or
+    EXPIRED). It creates no new hold, changes nothing, and does not move
+    `expires_at`.
+19. "Same request" compares requested items, not held items. A retry of a
+    partially filled reservation with the original request matches. A request
+    equal to what was held does not, unless it is also equal to what was
+    requested.
+20. Calling `reserve` with a key already used by a successful `reserve`, but
+    with a different warehouse or different requested items, raises
+    `IdempotencyConflict`. This applies even if the original reservation is
+    CONFIRMED, RELEASED or EXPIRED.
+21. Keys are global (not per warehouse) and never expire.
+22. A `reserve` call that raised leaves no trace. Its key can later be used
+    for any request.
+
+### Confirm
+
+23. `confirm_reservation` on an ACTIVE, unexpired reservation places an
+    ordinary PLACED `Order` in the same warehouse, for exactly the units in
+    `Reservation.items`. After a partial hold, the order covers only those
+    units. The order uses the normal order ID sequence and has `placed_at`
+    equal to the confirm time.
+24. Afterwards the reservation has status `CONFIRMED` and `order_id` set to
+    the new order's ID. The returned value is that order.
+25. Confirming does not change `available` for any SKU (the hold becomes an
+    allocation) or on-hand. On-hand moves only when the order ships.
+26. The resulting order behaves like any other order: it can be shipped or
+    cancelled with `ship_order` / `cancel_order`.
+27. Confirming a reservation that is already CONFIRMED returns the same order
+    (same `order_id`) and creates nothing. This holds at any time, including
+    after `expires_at`.
+28. Confirming an expired reservation, including one that hit `expires_at`
+    exactly, raises `ReservationExpired`. No order is created.
+29. Confirming a RELEASED reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`. No
+    order is created.
+
+### Release
+
+30. `release_reservation` on an ACTIVE, unexpired reservation sets its status
+    to `RELEASED` and frees its units at once. It returns the reservation.
+31. Releasing a reservation that is RELEASED or EXPIRED is a no-op. It returns
+    the reservation unchanged, without error.
+32. Releasing a CONFIRMED reservation raises `InvalidReservationState`. The
+    reservation and its order are unchanged.
+
+### Lookup
+
+33. `get_reservation` returns the reservation with its current status. A
+    lapsed ACTIVE reservation is reported as `EXPIRED`. Expired reservations
+    stay retrievable.
+34. An unknown reservation ID raises `ReservationNotFound` from
+    `get_reservation`, `confirm_reservation` and `release_reservation`.
+
+### Direct orders
+
+35. `place_order` can take only available stock (criterion 6), so direct
+    orders compete with holds. It stays all-or-nothing, exactly as today, and
+    raises `InsufficientStock` if any line exceeds available. Partial filling
+    applies to reservations only.
+
+### Compatibility
+
+36. The existing test suite passes unchanged.
+37. No package code reads the wall clock. All timestamps and expiry decisions
+    use the injected `Clock`.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Extending or refreshing a hold.
+- A sweep, scheduler or background expiry job.
+- Showing held units in `allocated()` or as a column in `stock_report`.
+- Blocking `receive` / `adjust` because of holds, or shrinking or cancelling
+  reservations when stock is written off.
+- Partial filling for `place_order`.
+- Changing a reservation's items after creation.
+- Expiring idempotency keys.
+- Linking an order cancellation back to its reservation.
+
+## Left to the implementer
+
+- If a write-off took on-hand below a hold, `confirm_reservation` may either
+  succeed or raise `InsufficientStock`.
+- When the reservation is CONFIRMED and its order is later cancelled or
+  shipped, what the reservation shows (it may simply stay CONFIRMED).
+- Whether the idempotency check for a reused key runs before or after
+  validation of the request.
+- Which SKU `InsufficientStock` reports when no line can hold anything.
+- Whether an empty idempotency key is accepted. If it is rejected, the error
+  is an `InventoryError` and, like any rejected request, leaves no trace.
